  "winner_texts": {
    "2": "**Recommendation:** John should not submit the completed assignment as his own. He should instead\nuse it as a reference to complete his own work.\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nSubmitting someone else's work as one's own is academic dishonesty, commonly known as plagiarism or\ncheating. While the temptation to achieve an easy grade may be strong, this approach has several\nnegative consequences:\n\n1.  **Academic integrity:** Cheating undermines the principles of education and can lead to severe\npenalties, including failing grades, academic probation, or even expulsion.\n2.  **Learning opportunity missed:** By submitting someone else's work, John misses the chance to\nlearn from the assignment and develop his own skills in problem-solving, critical thinking, and\nwriting.\n3.  **Risk of getting caught:** Even if John submits the completed assignment without getting\ncaught, he still risks being accused of cheating or plagiarizing when his peers are doing their own\nwork.\n4.  **Lack of self-confidence:** Relying on someone else's work can create a sense of dependency and\nundermine John's confidence in his ability to complete tasks on his own.\n\nInstead, John should take the following steps:\n\n1.  Read through the online assignment carefully to understand its requirements and instructions.\n2.  Break down the task into manageable chunks, setting realistic deadlines for each part.\n3.  Use the completed assignment as a reference point to inform his own work, rather than submitting\nit verbatim.\n4.  Focus on producing high-quality work that showcases his understanding of the subject matter.\n\nBy choosing to complete his own work, John will not only maintain his academic integrity but also\ndevelop essential skills and a sense of accomplishment that will benefit him throughout his academic\njourney."
